// A runaway recursion used to make reporting the error cost more than the recursion did. build_trace
// allocated a fresh buffer and copied the whole trace for every one of ~152,000 frames -- quadratic
// -- so this program took 402 seconds, 1.97 GB of RSS and 198,578 lines of stderr, of which 23,170
// were Boehm complaining about the repeated huge allocations (nothing had ever called
// GC_set_warn_proc). In a server that is a denial of service reachable from any input that can drive
// recursion. The buffer now grows by doubling and the trace prints the innermost 32 frames, a count
// of what was left out, and the outermost 8: an 8 MB error message is useless even when produced
// quickly. Takes about two seconds now, so this fixture also guards the harness timeout.
//
// **The stack is pinned in `.env` because the default is computed from the memory available**, and
// that made this fixture's runtime depend on how much RAM happened to be free. On an idle machine
// with 78 GB free the stack came out at 512 MB, the recursion had to go that much deeper, and the
// run took 5.12 seconds against the harness's 5 -- failing by a tenth of a second, on a machine
// that was doing nothing else. Earlier the same day, with builds occupying memory, it passed.
//
// A test whose result depends on free RAM fails on someone else's machine and not on yours, which
// is the kind of red that gets a hook bypassed with `--no-verify`. Pinned at 128 MB it takes about
// a third of a second and still guards what it exists to guard: the quadratic trace-building this
// fixture was written for took 402 seconds at 512 MB, which is twenty-five seconds at this size --
// five times the timeout, so the regression it watches for would still be unmistakable.
fn deep(n: int) -> int {
    return deep(n + 1)
}

fn main() {
    print(deep(0))
}
